# Capturing {{TITLE}} insight

## Two hard rules

1. **Write only, never read.** Do not open, list, or grep `{{STORE}}/`, not even to check
   for duplicates. It holds unmoderated material. A duplicate is the human's to merge.
2. **Never promote a note into a published library**, even when asked directly. That is a
   manual moderation step. Decline and say so.

## One test

Before anything else, ask: **did the human just tell me something I would not have
produced?** A correction, a constraint I did not know, a reason my suggestion was wrong.

That is a collision, and it is the only thing worth writing down. The subject does not
matter. What I already knew and merely restated does not qualify, and neither does
agreement.

Then ask: **would this still be true in a year?** If it would be stale in six months it
is too concrete. State the principle as the rule. Versions, product names and measured
values are evidence, and they go in *Origin*.

{{DURABLE_CONTRAST}}

## How much they put in

This is the scale the whole decision hangs on, so decide it before anything else.

| `evidence` | |
|---|---|
{{EVIDENCE_TABLE}}

"Yes, because the retry has to be idempotent" is evidence. "Yes, good" is not. When
unsure between endorsement and acceptance, it was acceptance.

**{{EVIDENCE_FREE}} is never rationed.** A collision the human actually pushed back on is
what this store exists for, and the allocator will not refuse one however many notes the
day already holds. Weaker evidence is what the daily cap is there to ration, and an
acceptance is not a collision at all -- the allocator refuses it outright.

So the question is never "have I written enough today". It is "how hard did they push
back", and a real correction is always worth the note.

## Write it

Ask the allocator for the path, naming the evidence. It counts, so you never list the
store:

```
{{ALLOCATE_CMD}} <kebab-slug> <{{EVIDENCE_NAMES}}>
```

Write that exact path:

```markdown
---
name: <kebab-slug>
description: <the rule, one line>
kind: {{KIND_NAMES}}
evidence: {{EVIDENCE_NAMES}}
scope: {{SCOPE_NAMES}}
captured: YYYY-MM-DD
source: <project>
---

<The rule as an imperative. One to three sentences.>

**Why:** <what concretely breaks without it>

**Origin:** <what produced it, in the human's own words where possible, plus any version
or measurement that served as evidence>
```

`kind` is what sort of collision it was:

{{KIND_TABLE}}

`scope` is the safety gate. When unsure, write `{{RESTRICTIVE_SCOPE}}`. If the rule holds
generally but the example does not, keep the rule and strip the example.

{{SCOPE_TABLE}}

`captured` is mandatory and always an absolute `YYYY-MM-DD` date taken from the current
context, never "today". `evidence` must match what you told the allocator -- the index
script checks the note and refuses the line if the two disagree.

Then add the index line with the script, never with the Write tool, which would require
reading `INDEX.md` first:

```
{{INDEX_CMD}} <NNNNN> <YYYY-MM-DD> <slug> <scope> <kind> <evidence> <hook text>
```

Finally tell the user the filename and scope in one sentence.

## Rate

{{SENSITIVITY_BAR}}

Weaker evidence is capped at **{{MAX_PER_DAY}} notes per day**; {{EVIDENCE_FREE}} is not
capped at all. If the allocator does refuse, say so in one sentence and carry on. Do not
work around it.

